    "solution": "Resource allocation table: \nPid Max \nX Y Z \nAlloc \nX Y Z \nNeed \nX Y Z \nAvail \nX Y Z \nP0 8 4 3 0 0 1 8 4 2  3 2 2 \nP1 6 2 0 3 2 0 3 0 0  \nP2 3 3 3 2 1 1 1 2 2  \n Request 1 needs [0, 0, 2] resources for process P 1, \nthen the system will become \nPid Max \nX Y Z \nAlloc \nX Y Z \nNeed \nX Y Z \nAvail \nX Y Z \nP0 8 4 3 0 0 3 8 4 0  3 2 0 \nP1 6 2 0 3 2 0 3 0 0  \nP2 3 3 3 2 1 1 1 2 2  \n Now, we can satisfy need of process P 1, after \nallotting resources to P1, \nPid Max \nX Y Z \nAlloc \nX Y Z \nNeed \nX Y Z \nAvail \nX Y Z \nP0 8 4 3 0 0 3 8 4 0  3 2 0 \nP1 6 2 0 3 2 0 3 0 0  6 4 0  \nP2 3 3 3 2 1 1 1 2 2  \n Now, with the given available resources, the need \nof neither P 2 nor P 0 can be satisfied, system is \nunsafe, Therefore, Req1 cannot be granted.  \n Now, checking for Req2. Request 2 needs [2, 0, 0] \nPid Max \nX Y Z \nAlloc \nX Y Z \nNeed \nX Y Z \nAvail \nX Y Z \nP0 8 4 3 0 0 1 8 4 2  1 2 2 \nP1 6 2 0 5 2 0 1 0 0   \nP2 3 3 3 2 1 1 1 2 2  \n P1’s need can be satisfied and available will become \nPid Max \nX Y Z \nAlloc \nX Y Z \nNeed \nX Y Z \nAvail \nX Y Z \nP0 8 4 3 0 0 1 8 4 2  1 2 2 \nP1 6 2 0 5 2 0 1 0 0  6 4 2  \nP2 3 3 3 2 1 1 1 2 2  \n Now, P 2’s request can be granted and after \ncompleting P2 the new available will become.  \nPid Max \nX Y Z \nAlloc \nX Y Z \nNeed \nX Y Z \nAvail \nX Y Z \nP0 8 4 3 0 0 1 8 4 2  1 2 2 \nP1 6 2 0 5 2 0 1 0 0  6 4 2  \nP2 3 3 3 2 1 1 1 2 2  8 5 3 \n With 8, 5, 3. Need of P0 can be fulfilled easily.  \n Hence Req2 can be granted.  \n(a)  System is unsafe\n∴  Req1 is Not granted  \n(b) < P1, P2, P0> \n System is safe\n Req2 is granted  \n \n\n \nDeadlocks",
